# TPLVAR_DISPLAY_NAME — Evaluation

Eval spec for the **TPLVAR_DISPLAY_NAME** use case. This folder is self-contained and owned
by this repo; the generic eval engine is installed as the `harness` package and imported —
never copied here.

**Target under test:** TPLVAR_TARGET_LABEL

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `run.sh` | Installs the engine if needed and runs the eval — the one-command entry point. |
| `spec.py` | Declares the target, datasets, and judges — the one file you edit. |
| `questions.csv` | `smoke` set — quick screen (`key,question,gold`). |
| `benchmark.csv` | `hard` set — graded benchmark (rubric columns; only `human_approval_status=approved` rows load). |

## 1. Configure

1. Set the endpoint in `spec.py` — `default_endpoint` (currently `TPLVAR_ENDPOINT`) is
   TPLVAR_ENDPOINT_HINT. Or override per run with `AGENT_ENDPOINT=...`. TPLVAR_TOKEN_NOTE
2. Edit `questions.csv` (the smoke set) and fill `benchmark.csv` (the hard set); mark
   benchmark rows `human_approval_status=approved` to include them.

## 2. Run

`run.sh` installs the shared engine into the active Python environment on first use, then
runs the eval:

```bash
./evaluation/run.sh --show    # inspect the resolved spec without calling the model
./evaluation/run.sh smoke     # quick smoke screen (holistic judges)
./evaluation/run.sh hard      # graded benchmark (rubric scoring + failure report)
./evaluation/run.sh           # default dataset (smoke)
```

**Engine location** — first match wins: `EVAL_ENGINE_PATH=/path/to/engine` (per machine),
then the path baked in at scaffold time (`TPLVAR_ENGINE_PATH`), then auto-detection of a
sibling checkout that provides `harness/`. To call the CLI directly instead:
`ai-eval --spec evaluation/spec.py <dataset>`.

**Results** land in `evaluation/eval-results/` (next to this spec, regardless of cwd):
- `<dataset>_report.md` — the analysis page: per failing/partial question it shows the
  question, expected answer, LLM answer, status, and failure reason (answers truncated for
  concision).
- `<dataset>_results.json` — the full table, each row keyed, with every `*/value` +
  `*/rationale`.

Override the location with `EVAL_RESULTS_DIR=/path`. Aggregate metrics also log to the MLflow
experiment (dashboard).

- `EVAL_SKIP_REPORT=1` — skip the markdown report (metrics + table still saved).
- `EVAL_REPORT_ANSWERS=1` — show expected/LLM answers full-length in the report (default
  truncates them; full text also lives in `<dataset>_results.json`).

## Runtime overrides (env)

| Var | Effect |
|---|---|
| `AGENT_ENDPOINT` | Override the endpoint/URL to hit. |
| `AGENT_AUTH_TOKEN` | Bearer token, if the endpoint needs one. |
| `JUDGE_MODEL` | Override the judge model. |
| `EVAL_ENGINE_PATH` | Where the eval engine checkout lives. |
| `EVAL_RESULTS_DIR` | Where results are written. |

## Judges

`Correctness`, `RelevanceToQuery`, and `no_hallucination` run automatically. Add more engine
built-ins by name (e.g. `"cites_source"`) or custom `Guideline(...)` objects in `spec.py`;
disable the default hallucination judge with `use_no_hallucination=False`.

**Judge cost — `judge_mode`.** Default `"separate"` runs one LLM call per judge (built-ins +
each guideline + rubric). Set `judge_mode="panel"` in `spec.py` to grade every criterion in
**one call per question** (N calls instead of N×judges); each criterion is still its own
dashboard metric. Trade-off: panel replaces the built-in Correctness/Relevance judges with
the engine's combined prompt, and a malformed judge reply costs the whole row (retried +
tolerantly parsed).
